*, this means I just drew a tile and now must discard one. So I need to assess: after drawing some tile (unknown), what discard keeps me in contention for an 8-point hand?\n\nHowever, since the actual drawn tile isn't specified, and only discards are listed as valid actions, we assume I\u2019ve already drawn, and now must choose a discard from the list.\n\nSo: I must pick the least harmful discard that preserves potential scoring elements.\n\nLet\u2019s analyze each discard option:\n\n1. **Play T7**: I have one T7 in hand. Discarding it breaks any chance of pairing it or making a bamboo chow around 5-6-7 or 7-8-9. But I already used T7 in a Chow. Keeping one T7 doesn\u2019t help much. Risk: low. But possible wait on T5/T6/T7 chows? Unlikely. Safe to discard.\n\n2. **Play F3 (West Wind)**: This is my **Seat Wind**. If I keep it, and later get a pair or pung, I gain 2 points. Currently I have one F3. If I discard it, I lose any chance of Seat Wind pattern. Since I already have F4\u00d72 (Prevalent Wind), keeping F3 gives chance for both wind bonuses. **High cost to discard**.\n\n3. **Play T6**: I have two T6s. Already used one in [T6,T7,T8] meld. Another T6 could be useful for another chow (e.g., 4-5-6 or 5-6-7), but I don\u2019t have those. With T4, T5, T6, I could have had a 4-5-6 chow, but I already used T6,T7,T8. Still, holding T6\u00d72 allows flexibility. But discarding one T6 is acceptable since I have redundancy.\n\n4. **Play W3**: Only one W3 in hand. W3 is rare (only 1 left unshown). But I have no other W tiles except W3. No chance for character suit chows. Not part of any strong pattern. Can be safely discarded.\n\n5. **Play T5**: Part of existing T4-T5 combo. Could support a 4-5-6 chow. But I already have a fixed Chow in Bamboo. With T4, T5, T6, I could theoretically form another chow. But I only have one T4 and T5. However, if I\u2019m aiming for **All Chows** (2 points), keeping these makes sense. Discarding T5 weakens sequence potential.\n\n6. **Play B4**: Only one B4. No other Dots except B4. Isolated. No meld potential unless I draw B3/B5 or B5/B6. B5 is scarce (only 1 left). Low utility. Safe to discard.\n\n7. **Play T4**: Similar to T5. Part of T4-T5-T6 possibility. But again, only one each. Losing T4 hurts any potential duplicate chow or shifted chow.\n\n8. **Play J3**: White Dragon. I have only one. No pung. Others may be collecting dragons. J2 was punged by opponent. J1 and J3 still somewhat available. But discarding J3 loses any future dragon pung possibility. However, **Dragon Pung** is only 2 points, and I\u2019d need two more J3s. Very unlikely. So low opportunity cost.\n\n9. **Play F4**: I have **two F4s (North Wind)**. This is **Prevalent Wind**, so if I keep a pung of F4, I get 2 points. Discarding one F4 reduces me to one \u2014 kills any chance of pung. High cost. Should avoid discarding F4 unless necessary.\n\nNow compare strategic goals:\n- I may be heading toward **All Chows** (2 points) + possibly **Mixed Double Chow** or **Short Straight**.\n- I have one exposed Chow already.\n- Remaining hand: W3, T4, T5, T6\u00d72, T7, B4, F3, F4\u00d72, J3\n- After discarding, I need to maintain potential for at least 8 points.\n\nBest chance:\n- If I keep T4, T5, T6, T7 \u2192 can form multiple chows: e.g., T4-T5-T6 and T5-T6-T7 (overlap), but not possible simultaneously.\n- Alternatively, if I draw T8, I could have T6-T7-T8 again? But already used.\n- More likely: aim for **Mixed Shifted Chows** or **Mixed Triple Chow**, but not strong.\n- Better: minimize loss of scoring chances.\n\nCritical insight:\n- I have **F4\u00d72** \u2014 very close to a pung. F4 appears 4 times unshown, none visibly taken. Previous players haven\u2019t shown interest. Possible another F4 is coming.\n- Similarly, **F3** is my seat wind. If I keep both F3 and F4\u00d72, I could potentially get a pair of F3 and pung of F4, giving both **Prevalent Wind** and **Seat Wind** (2+2=4 points).\n- So **keep F3 and F4s**.\n\nThus, **do NOT discard F3 or F4**.\n\nAmong others:\n- **T6\u00d72**: redundant. I already used T6 in a Chow. One extra T6 is enough for flexibility. Discard one T6 is acceptable.\n- But better to keep T4, T5, T6, T7 for chow potential.\n\nWait \u2014 I already have an exposed Chow [T6,T7,T8]. That counts as one meld. The rest of my hand needs three more melds and a pair.\n\nCurrent hand structure:\n- Meld 1: [T6,T7,T8] (exposed)\n- Hand: W3, T4, T5, T6, B4, F3, F4\u00d72, J3\n\nIf I discard:\n- **T6**: then left with T4, T5 \u2014 possible 4-5-6 if draw T6, but T6 still available.\n- **T7**: only one, not useful anymore.\n- **B4/W3/J3**: isolated, no support.\n\nBut consider **Outside Hand** (8 points): requires every meld and pair to contain terminals or honors.\n\nCurrently:\n- My meld [T6,T7,T8] contains T6 (simple), T7 (simple), T8 (simple) \u2192 all simples! So this meld **disqualifies** Outside Hand.\n\nSo **Outside Hand is impossible**.\n\nSimilarly, **All Chows** (2 points) is possible if the rest of my hand forms chows and a suited pair.\n\nBut I have W3 (terminal), B4 (simple), F3/F4/J3 (honors) \u2014 hard to make chows.\n\nAlternatively, **Half Flush**? Not really \u2014 too many honors.\n\nBest bet: **Melded Hand** (6 points): all melds completed by stealing, and winning tile from opponent. But I already did Chi, so yes, possible.\n\nBut need total \u22658 points.\n\nPossible patterns:\n- Prevalent Wind (F4 pung): 2 points\n- Seat Wind (F3 pung): 2 points\n- Dragon Pung (J3): 2 points\n- All require pungs.\n\nBut I have only singles.\n\nAlternatively, **Two Concealed Pungs** (2 points) \u2014 but I have no concealed pungs.\n\nWait \u2014 my only chance for points is through **winds/dragons** or **chows-based** patterns.\n\nAnother idea: **Big Three Winds** (12 points): three pungs of wind tiles.\n\nI have F3\u00d71, F4\u00d72. If I can get F1 or F2, but F2 is gone (0 left), F1 has 2 left.\n\nBut I\u2019d need to collect F1 or F3.\n\nF3 is West, my seat wind. F4 is North, prevalent.\n\nSo if I can get F3\u00d73 and F4\u00d73, plus another wind pung.\n\nBut F1 (East) has 2 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nBut very unlikely.\n\nBetter to focus on safety and preserving options.\n\nNow, among the discard choices, the **least valuable tiles** are those that cannot contribute to any scoring pattern.\n\n- **B4**: Dot-4, simple, no connections, only one \u2014 useless.\n- **W3**: Character-3, terminal, but isolated.\n- **T7**: only one, already used in chow, no further use.\n- **J3**: White Dragon, but only one \u2014 unless I draw two more, useless.\n\nBut **F3 and F4 are more valuable** than B4, W3, J3.\n\nBetween B4, W3, T7, J3 \u2014 which to discard?\n\nNote: I already discarded B5, B1, B9 \u2014 so opponents may think I\u2019m not collecting Dots.\n\nSimilarly, I played W7, W3, W8 \u2014 so not collecting Characters.\n\nSo discarding B4 or W3 is safe.\n\nBut **J3**: White Dragon. Opponents may be collecting dragons. J2 was punged, so someone has it. J1 and J3 less active.\n\nDiscarding J3 might be safe.\n\nBut **J3 is worth 2 points if I ever get a pung**. Same as F3 or F4.\n\nBut I have only one J3, same as F3.\n\nBut I have **two F4s** \u2014 so higher chance to complete pung.\n\nTherefore, **F4\u00d72 > F3 > J3** in value.\n\nSo priority to keep F4, then F3, then maybe J3.\n\nThus, **do not discard F4 or F3**.\n\nSimilarly, T6\u00d72: I can afford to discard one T6.\n\nBut let's see the **valid actions** include **Play T6**, which means discard one T6.\n\nThat seems reasonable.\n\nBut also **Play B4**, **Play W3**, **Play T7**, etc.\n\nNow, **T4 and T5**: together they can form part of a chow. With T6, they can make 4-5-6. But I already have a T6.\n\nBut if I keep T4, T5, T6, I have a potential chow.\n\nBut I need to form three more melds.\n\nMore importantly, if I'm going for **All Chows** (2 points), I need all four melds to be chows and a suited pair.\n\nI already have one chow.\n\nRemaining hand must form three chows and a pair.\n\nBut I have F4\u00d72 \u2014 that could be the pair!\n\nYes! **F4\u00d72** can be the pair.\n\nThen the rest must form three chows.\n\nCurrent hand: W3, T4, T5, T6, T7, B4, F3, J3\n\nTo form chows:\n- T4-T5-T6: possible\n- T5-T6-T7: possible\n- But overlapping.\n\nAlso need third chow.\n\nW3? Only one.\n\nB4? Only one.\n\nF3? Honor, no chow.\n\nJ3? Same.\n\nSo impossible to get three more chows.\n\nThus, **All Chows is impossible**.\n\nSo no point keeping T4/T5/T6/T7 for chow-only strategy.\n\nTherefore, **chance of scoring via chows is low**.\n\nBetter to pivot to **Pungs of Honors**.\n\nSo maximize chance to get pungs of F3, F4, J3.\n\nI have:\n- F4\u00d72 \u2192 one away from pung\n- F3\u00d71 \u2192 two away\n- J3\u00d71 \u2192 two away\n\nSo **F4 is closest**.\n\nThus, **must keep F4\u00d72**.\n\n**F3 is second most important**.\n\n**J3 is lowest priority honor**.\n\nSimilarly, **T6\u00d72**: I can discard one.\n\nBut do I need T6? Only for chows, which aren't working.\n\nAlternatively, **Mixed Double Chow** (1 point): two identical chows in different suits.\n\nBut I have only one suit with chows: Bamboo.\n\nNo other suit has chows.\n\nImpossible.\n\n**Short Straight**: two consecutive chows in same suit, like 2-3-4 and 3-4-5? No.\n\nNot feasible.\n\nSo no chow-based pattern is reachable.\n\nTherefore, **best hope is honor pungs**.\n\nPrioritize:\n- Keep F4\u00d72 (Prevalent Wind, 2 points)\n- Keep F3 (Seat Wind, 2 points)\n- Keep J3 (Dragon, 2 points)\n\nBut I must discard one.\n\nSo which honor is least likely?\n\nF4 has highest potential (already two).\n\nF3: only one, but seat wind.\n\nJ3: dragon, but no special bonus.\n\nBut **Dragon Pung** is 2 points, same as Seat/Prevalent Wind.\n\nBut I have no other dragons.\n\nSo all are equal in point value, but F4 is closest to completion.\n\nSo **keep F4\u00d72 at all costs**.\n\nThus, **do not discard F4**.\n\nSimilarly, **discard a non-honor single tile**.\n\nOptions: B4, W3, T7, T5, T4, T6, J3\n\nJ3 is honor, so better to discard a suited simple tile.\n\nBest candidates: **B4**, **W3**, **T7**\n\nAmong them, **B4** is weakest \u2014 no connection, and I've discarded other B tiles.\n\nSimilarly, W3 is terminal, but I have no other Ws.\n\nT7: I already used in chow, and have no other T7s.\n\nBut T7 is part of a 7-8-9 possibility? But T8 is in my meld, and I don't have T9.\n\nNo.\n\nSo all are weak.\n\nBut **T6\u00d72**: I have two, so discarding one T6 is less harmful than losing a unique tile.\n\nAh! Key point: **I have two T6s**, so discarding one leaves one.\n\nWhereas T7, T5, T4, B4, W3, F3, J3 \u2014 only one each.\n\nSo **discarding one T6** sacrifices redundancy, but keeps flexibility.\n\nWhereas discarding T7 removes it entirely.\n\nSame for others.\n\nSo **Play T6** is safer than Play T7 or Play T5.\n\nBut I only need one T6 anyway.\n\nSo **Play T6** is acceptable.\n\nBut is there a better candidate?\n\nWhat about **Play J3**?\n\nJ3 is a dragon, but I have only one, and no way to get more.\n\nF4\u00d72 is better.\n\nBut if I must choose between discarding J3 or T6, both are acceptable.\n\nBut **J3 has scoring potential**, while T6 does not (already used in chow, and no flush possible).\n\nSo **better to discard T6 than J3**.\n\nWait: I have two T6s. Discarding one leaves one T6.\n\nI have one J3. Discarding J3 loses it forever.\n\nBut the **value of J3** is only if I draw two more \u2014 extremely unlikely.\n\nWhereas T6 might help in a chow, but also unlikely.\n\nBut in terms of **tile efficiency**, discarding a duplicate is better.\n\nStandard strategy: when in doubt, discard duplicates first.\n\nHere, **T6 is the only duplicated tile**.\n\nSo **Play T6** is the safest move.\n\nIt maintains all honor singleton options (F3, F4\u00d72, J3) and keeps one T6 for flexibility.\n\nOther discards would break uniqueness.\n\nTherefore, best choice is **Play T6**.\n\nReason: Preserves all potential scoring elements (especially F4\u00d72 for Prevalent Wind), avoids breaking unique tiles, and discards a redundant tile.\n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
