exposed melds \\u2014 so impossible.\\n\\nAny pattern with concealed requirement is hard because I have exposed melds.\\n\\n**Fully Concealed Hand** (4 pts) impossible due to Peng and Chi.\\n\\n**Concealed Hand** (2 pts) also impossible because I took tiles from others.\\n\\nSo must go for exposed patterns.\\n\\n**Melded Hand** (6 pts): all melds completed by taking from others, and pair completed by taking from others.\\n\\nI have Peng F4 \\u2014 so took F4 from someone.\\n\\nChi T7 \\u2014 took T7 from previous player.\\n\\nSo both melds are exposed and taken.\\n\\nThen if I make two more melds by taking, and pair by taking, I can get Melded Hand (6 pts).\\n\\nBut I need to form two more melds by Chi or Peng.\\n\\nAnd win by stealing.\\n\\nBut currently, I have to discard.\\n\\nNow, for safety and efficiency, best to discard a safe tile.\\n\\nB4 is very safe.\\n\\nW3 is riskier.\\n\\nF3: seat wind, might be dangerous.\\n\\nJ3: dragon, always dangerous.\\n\\nT4,T5,T6,T7: middle dots, relatively safe, but could complete chows.\\n\\nBut since I need to keep most, better to discard a non-dot, non-honor.\\n\\nSo B4 or W3.\\n\\nB4 is safer.\\n\\nMoreover, B4 is not useful for any major pattern.\\n\\nWhereas W3 could be part of lower chows, but unlikely.\\n\\nSo I think Play B4 is best.\\n\\nBut let's confirm the answer format.\\n\\nValid actions include Play B4.\\n\\nSo I'll go with that.\\n\\nFinal decision:\\n\\nSituation: I have an exposed chow in dots [T6,T7,T8] and a pung of North Wind (F4). My hand contains middle dots T4-T7, making a possible chow [T4,T5,T6] or [T5,T6,T7], along with singleton honors F3 (seat wind) and J3 (dragon), and two off-suit tiles W3 and B4. Other players have discarded B4 multiple times, making it very safe. W3 is riskier as the across player has a chow ending in W3 and might be collecting.\\n\\nEvaluation: Discarding B4 or W3 is optimal to move toward a Half Flush or Melded Hand. Among options, B4 is the safest discard due to frequent discards by the previous player. Keeping T4-T7 preserves flexibility for dot-based chows. Honors F3 and J3 have pattern potential but are riskier to discard.\\n\\nReason: B4 is highly unlikely to complete any opponent's hand, given its repeated discard history, while removing a non-dot tile helps streamline toward a Half Flush or other scoring pattern.\\n\\nAnswer: Play B4\"}"}
