ort Straight is for two chows like 234+567 \u2014 here 456+678 \u2192 shares T6, so not pure. But **Mixed Shifted Chows (6 points)** requires three consecutive chows in different suits shifted by one \u2014 not applicable.\n\nAlternatively, **Pure Double Chow (1 point)**: two identical chows in same suit. Not there.\n\nBut if I keep T4 and T5, and draw T3 or T6, I could make [T3,T4,T5] or [T4,T5,T6].\n\nBut previous player has [T3,T4,T5] already exposed \u2014 so T3, T4, T5 have been used \u2014 only 2 T4, 2 T5 left. Possible, but risky.\n\nAnother idea: **Half Flush (6 points)** \u2014 one suit + honors. I have many Wongs? No. Mostly scattered.\n\nBut I have [F4,F4,F4] and other winds: F3 (West), and Seat Wind is West \u2192 if I keep F3, I could aim for F3 pair or even pung.\n\nIf I draw another F3, I get a pair. If someone discards F3, I can Peng.\n\nCurrently, F3 has 3 unshown \u2014 very likely available.\n\nMoreover, **Seat Wind (2 points)**: pung of F3 (West) \u2192 worth 2 points.\n\nSo if I keep F3, I have a chance to complete Seat Wind pattern.\n\nCurrently I have:\n- Prevailing Wind (F4 pung): 2 points\n- All Types: 6 points\n- Melded Hand (if I win off discard): 6 points\n\nIf I also get Seat Wind (F3 pung): +2 \u2192 total 16, plus possibly more.\n\nBut I need to complete hand.\n\nBest strategy: improve hand flexibility.\n\nCompare discards:\n\n- **Discard W4**: weak tile, no use, not contributing to anything. Safe, but no loss.\n- **Discard B7**: similar.\n- **Discard B4**: only one, not helpful.\n- **Discard J3**: dangerous? J3 has 4 unshown \u2014 no one has played it \u2014 maybe someone is hoarding. But discarding it might give it away. But I have no use for it unless I draw more.\n- **Discard T4 or T5**: breaks potential T4-T5-T6 sequence. But I already have a T6 in hand and used in exposed chow \u2014 wait, no: the exposed chow [T6,T7,T8] was formed earlier \u2014 so those tiles are gone from hand. The T6 in hand is separate? Wait \u2014 no: when you form a meld via Chi, you place three tiles down. You can still have another T6 in hand.\n\nYes \u2014 possible to have multiple copies.\n\nI have one T6 in hand, and the meld [T6,T7,T8] uses one T6 \u2014 so total two T6 used. Two T6 remain unshown \u2014 okay.\n\nSo hand: T4, T5, T6 \u2014 can form [T4,T5,T6] if I keep them.\n\nThat would give me two chows in Bamboo: [T4,T5,T6] and [T6,T7,T8] \u2014 overlapping, but allowed.\n\nThen I\u2019d have two concealed chows? No: [T6,T7,T8] is exposed via Chi, so not concealed.\n\nBut still, two chows in same suit.\n\nWith T4,T5,T6 \u2192 [T4,T5,T6] concealed.\n\nThen possible **Pure Shifted Chows (24 points)**? No \u2014 that requires four successive chows with fixed interval.\n\nOr **Pure Double Chow (1 point)** \u2014 if I have two identical chows \u2014 not here.\n\nBut **Short Straight (1 point)**: two chows in same suit with consecutive numbers, e.g., 234 and 567. Here, 456 and 678 \u2192 not consecutive (missing gap), but 456 and 678 share 6 \u2014 not valid for Short Straight.\n\nShort Straight requires non-overlapping, like 123+456 or 234+567.\n\nSo 456+678 does not qualify.\n\nThus, no major pattern.\n\nBetter to focus on honor pair.\n\nBest candidate for pair: **F3 (West Wind)** \u2014 my seat wind.\n\nIf I keep F3, and draw another F3, I get a valuable pair. Then, if I complete three other melds, I can win.\n\nOther singles: W4, B4, B7, T4, T5, T6, J3 \u2014 all lonely.\n\nJ3: White Dragon \u2014 if I keep it, and draw another, I get a pair \u2014 but no pattern bonus unless I pung it.\n\nBut Dragon Pung is 2 points \u2014 but I don\u2019t have it.\n\nCurrently, **All Types (6)** and **Melded Hand (6)** are achievable.\n\nTo maximize chances, keep **F3** for potential Seat Wind pair.\n\nDiscard something less promising.\n\nOptions to discard: W4, B4, B7, T4, T5, T6, J3.\n\nAmong these, **B7** is weakest \u2014 no sequence, not terminal-heavy, not part of known patterns.\n\nSimilarly, **W4** is medium.\n\nBut **T4 and T5** \u2014 if I keep them, I can try to form [T3,T4,T5] if I draw T3, but previous player has [T3,T4,T5] meld \u2014 so they have three T3s \u2014 T3 count is 0 \u2014 **no T3 left**!\n\nUnshown T3: 0 \u2192 so **cannot draw T3**.\n\nTherefore, **cannot form [T3,T4,T5]**.\n\nSimilarly, T2: 0 left.\n\nSo only possible chow with T4-T5 is [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 which I can make if I keep all three.\n\nAnd T6 has 2 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nSo T4, T5, T6 together can form [T4,T5,T6].\n\nThat\u2019s a valid concealed chow.\n\nThen I\u2019d have:\n- [T6,T7,T8] exposed chow\n- [T4,T5,T6] concealed chow\n- [F4,F4,F4] exposed pung\n- Need one more meld and a pair.\n\nStill need more.\n\nBut better than nothing.\n\nAlternatively, keep F3 and J3 for honor pair potential.\n\nBut only one of each.\n\nBest discard: **B7** \u2014 least useful, not part of any sequence, not honor, not helping All Types (we already have Dots via B4,B7).\n\nSimilarly, B4 is also weak.\n\nBut B4 and B7 are both Dots \u2014 if I keep both, could form pair if I draw another, but B4 has 1 left, B7 has 3 \u2014 possible.\n\nBut B7 is more common.\n\nBut neither helps specific pattern.\n\nBack to **Seat Wind**: F3.\n\nIf I keep F3, and later draw F3, I can have a pair of West Wind \u2014 which qualifies for **Seat Wind (2 points)** if I have the pung, but pair alone doesn\u2019t count \u2014 **Seat Wind pattern requires a pung or kong of seat wind**.\n\nCheck: \"Seat Wind: A hand with a pung or kong of the seat wind of the current player.\"\n\nSo **pair is not enough** \u2014 must be pung or kong.\n\nSo to get 2 points, I need **three F3s**.\n\nCurrently I have one F3, two unshown F3s? Unshown F3: 3 \u2014 yes, 3 left \u2192 possible.\n\nBut hard to get.\n\nSimilarly, Prevailing Wind F4 \u2014 I already have pung \u2014 locked in 2 points.\n\nSo Seat Wind is possible but difficult.\n\nBut still, F3 is more valuable than B7.\n\nJ3: Dragon \u2014 if I get three, I get Dragon Pung (2 points) \u2014 but same issue.\n\nBut I already have 2 points from Prevailing Wind.\n\nNow, **All Types (6)** is solid.\n\n**Melded Hand (6)**: I already have two exposed melds (Chi and Peng), so as long as I don\u2019t form a concealed meld, and I win by stealing a discard, I get it.\n\nIf I draw the winning tile, I lose Melded Hand, but gain Self Drawn (1 point) \u2014 net loss.\n\nSo better to win by discard.\n\nTherefore, I should avoid self-drawing win.\n\nBut for now, focus on improving hand.\n\nBest chance: aim for **Half Flush**? I have many honors? No.\n\nOr **Full Flush**? No \u2014 too scattered.\n\nBest is to complete a second chow in Bamboo.\n\nKeep T4, T5, T6 to form [T4,T5,T6] when I draw another tile.\n\nBut I already have 14 tiles \u2014 I must discard one now.\n\nAfter discard, I\u2019ll have 13.\n\nThen next turn, I draw one \u2192 14 again.\n\nSo to form [T4,T5,T6], I need to keep T4, T5, T6.\n\nCurrently I have them.\n\nSo do **not** discard T4, T5, or T6.\n\nSimilarly, F3 and J3 \u2014 keep for honor potential.\n\nW4, B4, B7 \u2014 weaker.\n\nW4: only one, not helpful.\n\nB4 and B7: Dots \u2014 but no sequence.\n\nB4-B5-B6? B5 has 1 left, B6 has 2 \u2014 possible, but I don\u2019t have B5 or B6.\n\nNot feasible.\n\nSo weakest tiles: **W4, B4, B7**\n\nAmong them, **B7** is least connected \u2014 no adjacent tiles in hand.\n\nW4 could pair with nothing, but W4 has 2 unshown.\n\nBut no.\n\nI think **B7** is the safest and most expendable discard.\n\nIt doesn\u2019t contribute to any potential pattern.\n\nWhereas T4-T5-T6 can form a chow.\n\nF3 and J3 are honors \u2014 more likely to form pairs.\n\nW4 is character \u2014 we already have W4 in hand and others have W4-W5-W6, so W4 is floating.\n\nBut still, better than B7.\n\nSo discard B7.\n\nFinal decision: **Play B7**\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
